From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm6375: give the QUP UART its interconnect paths

qcom_geni_serial asks for QUP core bandwidth as soon as the port runs
faster than 115200:

	avg_bw_core = (baud > 115200) ? Bps_to_icc(CORE_2X_50_MHZ)
				      : GENI_DEFAULT_BW;
	port->se.icc_paths[GENI_TO_CORE].avg_bw = avg_bw_core;
	port->se.icc_paths[CPU_TO_GENI].avg_bw = Bps_to_icc(baud);
	geni_icc_set_bw(&port->se);

but sm6375's UART nodes declare no interconnects at all, so
geni_icc_get() finds no "qup-core" or "qup-config" path, every
geni_icc_set_bw() is a no-op and the request is silently dropped. The
sibling SoC this port is modelled on, sm6115, declares both paths on
each SE; sm6375 was simply never given them.

This was invisible for as long as nothing drove the NoC: with no
interconnect provider bound, the RPM keeps the bandwidth it handed over
at boot and the UART is fast enough by accident. Once a provider does
bind it aggregates the votes it is given, and a consumer that votes for
nothing gets nothing.

The Bluetooth controller sits on uart1 at 3.2 Mbps, so it is the first
thing to notice. Its firmware download starts failing:

	Bluetooth: hci0: QCA Downloading qca/crbtfw21.tlv
	Bluetooth: hci0: command 0xfc00 tx timeout
	Bluetooth: hci0: QCA Failed to send TLV segment (-110)

while the short EDL version query on the same link still succeeds, which
is what a starved QUP core looks like from userspace: small transfers
get through and large ones do not.

Bisected on the device between two boot images, same rootfs, 150
controller resets per image: an image whose kernel predates the
interconnect provider being built in passes 150/150, and the next one
fails within one or two.

Only uart1 is wired up here, because it is the only SE this board
enables and the only one that has been tested. The other SEs need the
same treatment before they are used.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375.dtsi
@@ -9,6 +9,8 @@
 #include <dt-bindings/dma/qcom-gpi.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/firmware/qcom,scm.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/interconnect/qcom,osm-l3.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/interconnect/qcom,rpm-icc.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/interconnect/qcom,sm6375.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/arm-gic.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/mailbox/qcom-ipcc.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/power/qcom-rpmpd.h>
@@ -1227,6 +1229,12 @@
 				operating-points-v2 = <&qup_opp_table>;
 				pinctrl-0 = <&qup_uart1_default>;
 				pinctrl-names = "default";
+				interconnects = <&clk_virt MASTER_QUP_CORE_0 RPM_ALWAYS_TAG
+						 &clk_virt SLAVE_QUP_CORE_0 RPM_ALWAYS_TAG>,
+						<&bimc MASTER_AMPSS_M0 RPM_ALWAYS_TAG
+						 &config_noc SLAVE_QUP_0 RPM_ALWAYS_TAG>;
+				interconnect-names = "qup-core",
+						     "qup-config";
 				status = "disabled";
 			};
 
